  "rawText": "Potato Soup\n\n2 tablespoons butter \nCouple glugs olive oil\n\n1 cup chopped onion\n1 cup chopped celery\n1 cup chopped carrots\n7-8 cloves garlic, minced\nBig shallot, minced \n2-3 tablespoons all-purpose flour\n\n4-5 potatoes, quartered (see below)\n\nCouple glugs dry white wine (Sauvignon Blanc)\n\n32 oz. vegetable broth\n1 1/2 cups whole milk (can sub up to 1/2 cup with heavy cream if you’re feeling spicy)\n12 oz. can evaporated milk\n\nHandful chopped fresh parsley\nHandful chopped fresh dill \n\n2 teaspoons Worcestershire sauce\n1 1/2 teaspoons mustard powder\n1 pinch ground allspice\n1 teaspoon celery seed\n1/2 teaspoon dried thyme\n1 teaspoon seasoning salt\n1/2 teaspoon sea salt (+ more to taste)\n1 teaspoon black pepper\nDash cayenne pepper\nCouple good pinches of truffle sea salt (optional but so good)\n\nCook potatoes\n\t⁃\tPut quartered potatoes in big pot. Cover with water. Salt it good. Cover and bring to a boil. Reduce and simmer for ~15 min. Strain. Set aside. \n\nHeat butter and oil in same big pot over medium high. Add garlic, shallot, onions and let them go for a few minutes. Add celery and carrots for a few more. Add flour and stir it up real good. Add potatoes, then the wine, broth, and milks, then everything else.\nLet it simmer on super low (or else it will burn to the bottom of the pot) for at least half an hour. Mash the potatoes as much as you want. Add more broth if it’s too thick.",
  "sourceNotes": "Source references '4-5 potatoes, quartered (see below)' but never specifies variety below — potato type is left to cook's judgment. The 1 1/2 teaspoons for mustard powder was lifted verbatim; recorded as 1.5 in qty with original phrasing in note.",
  "confidence": "high"
}
